o it is  we map that layer of atmosphere that I  showed you that was thicker here than it  was in the Arctic and we can treat it  just like a topographic map that you'd  use for hiking so here's a topographic  map of that layer of the atmosphere the  red colors are where the atmosphere is  thicker or the land would be higher and  the purple colors show where there'd be  a valley or the colder air and these  black lines indicate where the height of  that layer is the same so it's just like  on a topographic map for hiking if you  followed one of those lines you can stay  at a constant elevation and that's  what's being shown here as well so these  lines then we can use to track the shape  and size of these waves in the upper  level flow of the atmosphere you can see  those waves as you there's a big way big  northward swing in the jet stream the  jet stream is identified by where these  waves where these lines are very close  together that's where the wind speeds  are very strong and you can see that  there are places where there are big  dips in the jet stream so these big  north and south swings so we can look at  these maps like this every single day  and track the sizes of these waves so  here's a sort of zooming in on the on  the you're on the North Atlantic here  the same topographic map of the  atmosphere and what we see is one of  these big waves in the upper level flow  and in fact it's formed what we call a  blocking high and that's when we get one  of these Eddie's breaking off and  forming a circulation pattern in the  upper atmosphere and these are called  blocking because when they form they  tend to block the atmosphere in place  and lock it in place and the weather  patterns associated with that wave don't  change these create very very persistent  weather patterns there's been a lot of  focus on these blocking highs that over  the years because they are have such a  big impact on weather patterns in a  particular extreme weather  but what we've been 